WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:01.530
 Hi.

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.960
My name is Andy Park, and
I'm the Director of Visual

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.245
Development at Marvel Studios.

00:00:06.245 --> 00:00:08.860
Me and a group of artists,
we have the fun job

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.010
of designing the characters
that you see in the Marvel

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.750
Cinematic Universe.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.780
We thought it would be a
fun opportunity to give you

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:19.650
a little bit behind-the-scenes
glimpse into how we sketch,

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.780
how we design a character,
these heroes and villains.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.590
And, in this case, I'm
going to be drawing

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:28.250
someone that's very near
and dear to my heart,

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.150
Natasha Romanoff a.k.a.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:31.440
Black Widow.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.390
So check out this video.

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:35.370
Hopefully, afterwards,
you'll get a little bit

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:36.630
something out of it.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.570
We're going to make this
drawing that I do available

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:42.000
for download, so
feel free to color

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.030
it, to draw over
it, to trace it.

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:48.180
The whole point is to
learn and to have fun.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.560
So with that said,
let's get drawing.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:53.100
OK, let's get started.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:58.110
Normally, for my work, I use
Photoshop, Adobe Photoshop.

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:03.660
And I work digitally because
in my job as a concept artist

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.210
I'm always making
changes to my drawings,

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.330
to my designs, based
on the directors,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.280
or the producers, or
sometimes the actors.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.240
So I need to be able to
make changes very easily.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.860
So drawing digitally is just
the easiest way to make changes.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:24.930
You can draw traditionally
using pen and paper.

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:27.670
But it's always harder
to like, you know,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:29.910
you have to erase all the
time or do a new drawing.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:35.190
So I typically will do all
my concept illustrations

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:40.170
and my drawings in Photoshop
for my job at Marvel Studios.

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.890
So as you can see, this
is the Photoshop layout.

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:48.180
I'll usually start off about
8 and 1/2 by 11, pretty much,

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:52.570
or 8 by 11 inches, 300 DPI.

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:56.130
And I'll start sketching just
using, you know, a circle

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.740
brush that's squashed, you can
see in the lower right corner,

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.580
and about a 25% gray.

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:04.220
And then I'll start sketching.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.650
I'm going to start roughing out
whatever I'm trying to draw.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:11.550
In this case, I'm trying
to draw Black Widow.

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.860
And I'm thinking of
her in a, you know,

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:18.450
fairly not too
action-y of a pose.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.910
But I want her to
look like maybe she's

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:26.340
like walking forward or she's
walking down a corridor,

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.200
and she's seeking out
Taskmaster as you'll

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.190
see in the upcoming Black
Widow film, hopefully soon.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:40.230
So that's my thinking in
doing this initial sketch.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:42.060
So I'm just trying to
rough out, you know,

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:47.450
proportions, the size
of the head, the body,

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:51.510
the pose, the flow of the hair.

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.600
So a lot of times,
what you'll see me--

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.240
what is you're seeing
me do right now is just,

00:02:58.240 --> 00:02:59.710
you know, doing adjustments.

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:02.550
I can take a part
of the head and just

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:05.970
shrink it because I felt
like it was a little too big.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.850
And then I can make
some adjustments.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:12.030
You can also do
this in Procreate

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.770
if you have an iPad as well.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.960
So working digitally
has these advantages

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.880
where I can make
these adjustments

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.890
and make something
larger or smaller

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.830
on the fly versus if
I drew it on paper,

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.190
which is totally fine.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.650
I used to draw comics
for about 10 years

00:03:26.650 --> 00:03:28.680
where I would have to
erase and redraw it.

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:31.650
So either way it
works, but I'm just

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:35.680
taking advantage of the tools
that I have in Photoshop.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:38.400
So you know, I can
make some adjustments.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:40.970
I felt like the belt
was a little too high,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.980
and I added a little
bit of a curvature.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.280
So what you're seeing
is me just constantly

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:53.090
making adjustments to that
original sketch that I did.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.430
I feel like I have a
good base to work off of.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:57.950
Now, I'm just trying
to refine, you know,

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:03.410
side of the foot, the
details, going up and down

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:05.330
throughout the
figure to figure out.

00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:08.690
And then I can zoom
in and start doing--

00:04:08.690 --> 00:04:10.940
I don't want to go
detail at this point.

00:04:10.940 --> 00:04:15.320
I'm still trying to just
get the overall feel.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.290
I'm not thinking about lines.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:23.260
I'm thinking more about
just overall drawing.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:24.755
So I moved her head
a little bit forward

00:04:24.755 --> 00:04:27.140
because I felt like that would
make it a little bit more

00:04:27.140 --> 00:04:30.080
interesting than just
straight up and down.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.200
So going a little bit
forward on the body

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:36.240
makes for a little bit
more ready for whatever

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.910
is coming around the corner.

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.680
I lowered the opacity,
and I added a new layer

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.400
so that I can draw on top of
the old drawing that I just had,

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:47.660
that I just did.

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:52.010
And at this point, I am
adding a little bit of details

00:04:52.010 --> 00:04:55.130
on the costume now that
I have the general figure

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.780
kind of blocked in.

00:04:56.780 --> 00:04:58.590
And I'm trying to
figure out like,

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:03.560
OK, now I can start
drawing in the costume.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.480
Although, all this
while, I'm not

00:05:05.480 --> 00:05:06.920
thinking about pretty lines.

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:09.260
I'm not thinking about,
OK, I need to get all

00:05:09.260 --> 00:05:11.990
these lines to look beautiful.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.430
I am merely just, really, going
as fast as I can to just kind

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.190
of rough in all the details.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:20.720
And then from, you
know, later on, you

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:24.080
can start refining more
and more and start getting

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:25.460
more and more the pretty lines.

00:05:25.460 --> 00:05:28.310
I'm not necessarily going to
be doing that in this tutorial

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:31.230
because it's not about that.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.750
It's more about just my thinking
when it comes to drawing, how

00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:41.180
I go about going from a blank
page, start laying in details,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.930
and figuring out the
gesture, the character,

00:05:44.930 --> 00:05:50.820
the pose, the costume, and
all that kind of stuff.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:57.470
So for this tutorial, I am
drawing one of Black Widow's

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.870
costumes that you're
going to be seeing,

00:05:59.870 --> 00:06:04.760
eventually, in the theaters
from the Black Widow film.

00:06:04.760 --> 00:06:10.920
So this is one of her costumes
because she has a couple.

00:06:10.920 --> 00:06:14.670
And it's always fun to draw
because this is a character

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:19.860
that I had the honor of
designing ever since Avengers

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:21.200
way back in--

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:25.830
when I was working on it, it was
2010, the film came out 2012,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:32.000
but I designed all of
Natasha Romanoff's costumes

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.580
and all her film appearances
since the Avengers.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.300
And it's really been a pleasure
because this character,

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:42.163
played by Scarlett Johansson,
is just really interesting.

00:06:42.163 --> 00:06:44.130
It's always a character that
I've always wanted-- you know,

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.170
I think the audience,
myself included, always

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.090
wanted to know more about--

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:50.880
you know, we get
hints of her past.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.570
We get hints-- you know, she
talks about her red ledger

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:56.370
There's so many things.

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:58.800
And the way that Scarlett
Johansson portrays her

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.560
has just always been such
an interesting character.

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:06.270
And of course, if you've seen
that Infinity War and Endgame,

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.570
you see kind of the
culmination of her journey,

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:15.990
and it's such a heroic
gesture that she made.

00:07:15.990 --> 00:07:21.300
I don't want to spell it
out for those 10 people

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.870
on this earth who
haven't watched Endgame

00:07:23.870 --> 00:07:25.060
because they would be spoilers.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:28.730
But you know, for
those of you who

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.430
have watched it,
know what a heroic

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.380
act she made in that film.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:36.060
And she is the ultimate hero.

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:38.030
You know, that's her story.

00:07:38.030 --> 00:07:41.060
And of course, we're going
to be diving way deeper

00:07:41.060 --> 00:07:49.310
into her story, her back story,
who Natasha is as a person,

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:55.200
as a hero, from her
present to her past.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.520
And she's just a
really interesting

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:00.890
Avenger, a really
interesting person,

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:03.140
a really interesting character.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:06.650
So to be able to design
her costumes throughout all

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:11.990
these years, from Avengers
to her next appearance

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.450
in, what was it?

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:16.850
Captain America: Civil War.

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:18.180
I think I'm right.

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:19.850
I'm sorry, Captain
America: Winter

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:22.290
Soldier is what I meant to say.

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:29.660
And then, after that, was
Avengers: Age of Ultron, then

00:08:29.660 --> 00:08:34.850
Captain America: Civil War,
Avengers: Infinity War,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:39.200
and Avengers: Endgame and
then, in the upcoming solo

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:42.440
film, Black Widow.

00:08:42.440 --> 00:08:47.660
I think I named all of
her film appearances.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:53.590
So to be able to revisit
her look again for this film

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:54.390
was great.

00:08:54.390 --> 00:08:58.480
And you know, I'd love to
talk about my thinking behind

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:01.610
this costume maybe briefly.

00:09:01.610 --> 00:09:05.080
I can't go too into depth
because I don't want

00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:06.910
to spoil anything for the film.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:11.950
But you know, her costumes have
always been very utilitarian.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:17.230
It's all about practicality.

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:24.845
It's all about efficiency
and getting the job done.

00:09:24.845 --> 00:09:25.645
She's a spy.

00:09:30.300 --> 00:09:33.290
She's adaptable
in any situation.

00:09:33.290 --> 00:09:37.880
She's intelligent,
brave, and sneaky.

00:09:37.880 --> 00:09:40.270
[LAUGHS] And she's
got all the moves.

00:09:40.270 --> 00:09:46.430
So you know, she's got
that look from the comic.

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:47.405
It's just very sleek.

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:53.120
It's always pretty much black.

00:09:53.120 --> 00:09:54.470
That's why it was
fun in this film

00:09:54.470 --> 00:09:57.080
to be able to tackle
that suit that was

00:09:57.080 --> 00:09:59.210
the opposite, which is white.

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:04.490
So you know, she can't always
hide and be stealthy in black

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.310
because there's certain
situations where, you know,

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:11.210
she might have to blend in
with a white environment.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:18.170
So yeah, I'm drawing in all the
details from her Black Widow

00:10:18.170 --> 00:10:21.540
film costume, her latest one.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:25.160
This one has a
lot of gray in it.

00:10:25.160 --> 00:10:27.500
There's always black,
but this one probably

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:30.320
have more light
gray or medium gray

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:32.630
than any of her past films.

00:10:32.630 --> 00:10:36.290
And that was, you know, a
nod to one of her designs

00:10:36.290 --> 00:10:40.560
in the Marvel comics
back in the '80s.

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:44.140
So you know, I've always liked
that look with big collar,

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:46.520
gray costume.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:47.930
And the other
thing that I really

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:52.340
wanted to infuse into this
costume that she's never had

00:10:52.340 --> 00:10:54.170
but she's always
had in the comics

00:10:54.170 --> 00:11:00.410
was her gold gauntlets
and her gold belt.

00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:02.720
So those are two things
that I was really happy

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.460
that the director
and the producers

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:13.475
were totally happy to
allow her to have, gold.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.620
So maybe, in the past, gold
was just a little bit too

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:19.830
like, why gold?

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:21.640
This is not very stealthy.

00:11:21.640 --> 00:11:22.880
It kind of stands out.

00:11:22.880 --> 00:11:27.680
But I think we found a good,
happy medium in doing gold.

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:30.470
But it's, you know, not
bling gold, jewelry gold.

00:11:30.470 --> 00:11:32.830
It's more weathered.

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:35.270
It's more toned down.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.440
So it looks pretty
awesome in the film.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:40.580
So I was really happy
with the way it came out.

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:44.000
So yeah, I've been
drawing all the elements

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.245
from the guns to the hands.

00:11:45.245 --> 00:11:47.780
The guns in the
hands, as you notice,

00:11:47.780 --> 00:11:51.320
I drew last because
guns and her hands

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:54.000
are always, like, challenging,
and I want to get them right.

00:11:54.000 --> 00:11:57.230
So a lot of times,
I'll use reference.

00:11:57.230 --> 00:11:59.210
And in this case, I
had not shown it here.

00:11:59.210 --> 00:12:03.590
But I took photos of my hands
holding a toy gun so that I can

00:12:03.590 --> 00:12:05.130
just kind of get that right.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:07.010
And I'm making some
adjustments in the foot

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:09.590
because I felt like
it needs to look more

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.980
like she's walking for rather
than, you know, flat foot

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:14.090
in the back foot.

00:12:14.090 --> 00:12:15.600
And that looks a little better.

00:12:15.600 --> 00:12:17.660
You know, she's on
her toes in the back.

00:12:17.660 --> 00:12:20.300
I felt like her head was a
little weird tilted that way,

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:21.920
so I straightened it out.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.090
I darkened the entire drawing.

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:26.720
And again, as you
can tell, like,

00:12:26.720 --> 00:12:29.270
I'm not thinking about-- this
whole drawing tutorial is not

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:33.950
about how to do great, amazing
line work because it's really

00:12:33.950 --> 00:12:35.480
rough as you can still tell.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.330
But you know, it's got
enough detail that, you know,

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:39.530
you can tell what's going on.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:40.650
It's still interesting.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.295
But it's not, you
know, like a lot

00:12:43.295 --> 00:12:46.280
of times comics are like really
refined, nice, pretty line

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:47.410
drawings.

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:51.920
This, I would have to go another
step to make it really pretty.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.330
But you know, that's
not the point of this.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:55.760
It's more about how to draw.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:12:59.450
It's not so much how to
ink or do pretty line art.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:02.120
And this one I felt like
I need to add a little bit

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:06.260
of the tone, so that's why I
added the darks for the blacks

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.530
to suggest the blacks and
these grays for the grays.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:13.070
And then she has red hair,
and the Red color is dark.

00:13:13.070 --> 00:13:16.640
So that's why I felt like it
needed that to kind of have

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:18.710
this kind of completed look.

00:13:18.710 --> 00:13:22.580
And at this point, I felt like
I want to do just a little bit

00:13:22.580 --> 00:13:25.520
more detail and
refinement on the face,

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:27.580
so I zoomed in a little bit.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:28.970
I don't want to go too much.

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:34.040
And you know, I'm just going to
start cleaning up a little bit.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:36.950
This drawing is not about doing
a drawing of Scarlett Johansson

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:39.950
because it's clearly
not a likeness.

00:13:39.950 --> 00:13:43.430
But it's just more the essence
of the character Black Widow

00:13:43.430 --> 00:13:46.550
rather than the actress because
that would be a whole 'nother

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:47.510
tutorial in itself.

00:13:47.510 --> 00:13:50.180
But at this point, I'm trying
to just add a little bit

00:13:50.180 --> 00:13:53.150
more detail, interest
refining a little,

00:13:53.150 --> 00:13:54.380
because I felt like it's just--

00:13:54.380 --> 00:13:57.740
you know, the face is
the focus of any figure

00:13:57.740 --> 00:14:00.450
drawing that you do, character.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.690
So I just wanted to
make it a little nicer,

00:14:02.690 --> 00:14:06.595
adding little details, a
little bit of value color.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:16.090
I think that adds a lot in
refining just so that, you

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.320
know, when you see the drawing,
you know, the rest of it

00:14:18.320 --> 00:14:19.340
can be really rough.

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:21.170
And the face is still
going to stay rough.

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:23.300
But adding a little
bit more detail,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.820
I think, just adds so much more.

00:14:25.820 --> 00:14:27.680
So when you're
doing your drawings,

00:14:27.680 --> 00:14:29.840
just keep that in mind.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.660
People, we all tend
to look at the face

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.690
whenever we look at a drawing
if it's of a character,

00:14:35.690 --> 00:14:39.140
so you definitely want
the head and the face

00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:42.610
to really be strong.

00:14:42.610 --> 00:14:44.360
And the rest can be rougher.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.120
It doesn't have to be
as detailed as the rest.

00:14:47.120 --> 00:14:49.850
And it all kind of,
like, holds together.

00:14:49.850 --> 00:14:51.590
And here I'm kind of
just chiseling out.

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.020
I felt like there was
too much cranium, too

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:57.950
much hair on top and the sides.

00:14:57.950 --> 00:15:00.635
So that's just refining.

00:15:04.850 --> 00:15:11.020
And I really love just
drawing faces, painting faces.

00:15:11.020 --> 00:15:12.800
In my job, as a
concept designer,

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:16.030
I'm not really drawing
as much as I am painting.

00:15:16.030 --> 00:15:18.770
So what you're seeing
me do here is not really

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:21.830
something that I do
on my day-to-day job

00:15:21.830 --> 00:15:24.710
because, at this point, I'll
start painting or adding colors

00:15:24.710 --> 00:15:27.260
and using more brushstrokes.

00:15:27.260 --> 00:15:28.850
So there, you can
see the difference.

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:30.980
I just went on and
off with that layer

00:15:30.980 --> 00:15:33.900
to show the before and after.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:36.420
And so, at this point,
I feel pretty good.

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:38.060
I feel pretty good
about this drawing.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:42.710
I think it's, you know,
again, like I could spend

00:15:42.710 --> 00:15:46.010
another hour refining
this even more, adding

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:47.060
textures and all that stuff.

00:15:47.060 --> 00:15:52.310
But this is just to
get an overall drawing.

00:15:52.310 --> 00:15:54.380
You're seeing me flip
the canvas a lot.

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:57.050
And basically, that is
just to see my mistakes.

00:15:57.050 --> 00:15:58.940
When you see something in
a different perspective,

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:02.100
that's when you see, oh, one
eye is lower than the other.

00:16:02.100 --> 00:16:06.200
So I definitely advise you guys
to constantly flip your canvas

00:16:06.200 --> 00:16:07.970
or, you know, hold
it up to a light

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:09.800
or look at it in a mirror.

00:16:09.800 --> 00:16:13.040
I hope this has helped because
this is definitely a fun one,

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:15.470
a fun character for me to draw.

00:16:15.470 --> 00:16:19.550
I think, right there, I added
Scarlett Johansson's mole.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:20.630
Again, this is not--

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.790
I'm not claiming that
that's a great likeness

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:24.170
of Scarlett Johansson.

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:29.360
But if you were to see this
character or this drawing,

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:32.140
you know, most people would
say, oh, that's Black Widow.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:33.620
They would know who it is.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:35.570
They might not necessary say
that's Scarlett Johansson,

00:16:35.570 --> 00:16:37.110
but they'll know
it is Black Widow.

00:16:37.110 --> 00:16:39.600
So that's what I was
trying to accomplish.

00:16:42.820 --> 00:16:47.560
And the last thing that I'll
do and that everyone out there

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.300
should do after they
draw it-- they don't have

00:16:49.300 --> 00:16:54.060
to do all the time, but,
you know, 2020, Andy Park

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:55.970
is my signature.

00:16:55.970 --> 00:16:58.270
And yeah, there you go.

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:00.160
If you want to see
more of my drawings

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:03.460
and my paintings, the stuff
that I do at Marvel Studios,

00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:08.450
please check out my social
media @andyparkart on Instagram,

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:10.390
Twitter, and YouTube.

00:17:10.390 --> 00:17:12.819
Well, I hope to
see your drawings.

00:17:12.819 --> 00:17:17.140
I would love to see your take on
Black Widow or any other Marvel

00:17:17.140 --> 00:17:22.930
Studios hero or villain, and
please tag me because I'd

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:24.380
love to check it out.

00:17:24.380 --> 00:17:26.200
I'll try to make
whatever comments

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:27.940
or critique that I can.

00:17:27.940 --> 00:17:29.960
But keep drawing out there.

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:30.940
Stay safe.

00:17:30.940 --> 00:17:33.730
And I hope to see all of
your awesome drawings very,

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:34.670
very soon.

00:17:34.670 --> 00:17:35.970
Take care.

